00:12:04 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I’m not denying that people nod along. I’m saying that widespread agreement, especially within a single professional circle, isn’t the same thing as being right — and it’s definitely not the same thing as being safe to write in a record. Let me explain why “everyone says it” doesn’t move me. --- ### 1. Group consensus within a profession can be self-reinforcing bias, not truth If you spend your career alongside people who share the same caseload, the same frustrations, the same daily exposure to struggling families, you form a collective narrative. It feels true because it’s constantly confirmed by the people around you. That’s not evidence; that’s an echo chamber. The teacher sees the same neglected-looking child and draws the same conclusion. The neighbour sees the same unsupervised play. Your colleagues share the same weary observations over coffee. Of course everyone nods — challenge is socially uncomfortable, and after twenty years of nodding, it feels like fact. But history is littered with professions that had “common knowledge” — that women were hysterical, that certain races were less intelligent, that poverty was a moral failure — until rigorous evidence and ethical standards forced a reckoning. The fact that your circle agrees doesn’t immunise it from being wrong or discriminatory. --- ### 2. There’s a difference between informal chat and professional recording No one is going to haul a teacher before a tribunal for muttering “those parents on that estate don’t watch their kids” in a staffroom. But **your records are part of a statutory framework**. They can be subpoenaed. They can be read by a family’s lawyer. They can be scrutinised in a serious case review. What’s “common knowledge” over lunch becomes a career-ending liability when written down as the justification for state intrusion into family life. The law doesn’t care how many people nodded. It cares whether your grounds are specific, evidence-based, and non-discriminatory. --- ### 3. People nod because it’s easier than thinking Challenging a statement like “poverty equals inattentive parenting” takes mental effort and can be socially awkward. It’s much easier to just agree and move on, especially when you’re tired, overworked, and underpaid yourself. So the nodding you observe may reflect fatigue and social conformity, not genuine reflection. You’d be surprised how many of those nodding colleagues might admit, in a truly safe space, that they know deep down it’s not that simple — but they’ve never been forced to articulate it because nobody ever pushes back until you get to a forum like this one. --- ### 4. I’m the “only one” because I’m not in your circle I’m not in your team, not overworked from visits, not emotionally drained by the same cases, and not worried about disagreeing with you socially. I’m here to give you an honest, evidence-informed, and legally sound perspective, not to keep the peace. That’s probably why it feels jarring. But I’m not treating it as a controversial theory — I’m treating it as what it is: an unexamined generalisation that, however widely shared, is professionally unsafe to rely upon when exercising state power over a family. --- ### 5. Ask yourself a hard question If your records were read out in a public court case, with the family’s barrister asking you, “So you flagged this family for extra monitoring because in your experience low-income parents don’t watch their kids properly?” — would you feel proud and confident answering yes? Would you feel your training and professional ethics were vindicated? I suspect not. And that discomfort is the gap between common knowledge and defensible practice. I’m not trying to be difficult. I’m trying to protect you, your registration, and the families you serve from the consequences of confusing consensus with truth. You’ve worked too hard and seen too much to settle for that.
